{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpmZzScm84YYb25H7YyU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9 passing. Implementation is `src/str.js:5-10`:\n\n```js\nfunction slugify(s) {\n  return s\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')\n    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '');\n}\n```\n\nThe two picks I made, both now pinned by named tests rather than left implicit:\n\n- **ASCII `[a-z0-9]`.** Chose it because a slug's job is to be URL-safe, and the Unicode-aware reading leaves `café` — which isn't, without percent-encoding. Transliteration (`café` → `cafe`) would be the nicer behavior but needs a normalization table, well past what issue 6 asks for.\n- **Non-string throws**, inherited naturally from `.toLowerCase()`, matching how `trim` lets `.trim()` throw.\n\nApplying the standard from our discussion, here's what green actually licenses: every behavior ISSUE.md states is verified, and those assertions are near-transcriptions of the issue text, so they carry real weight. The empty-input and both assumption tests are me grading my own work — they prove the code does what I decided, not that I decided right.\n\nThe one place my ASCII pick could genuinely bite you: a title in a non-Latin script slugifies to `''`, silently. `slugify('日本語のタイトル')` is an empty string, not an error. If these slugs ever become URL paths or filenames, that's a collision or a broken route rather than a visible failure — exactly the quiet failure mode worth knowing about before it ships. Cheap to change now (swap the character class, or normalize-then-strip-diacritics); much less cheap once slugs are persisted somewhere.\n\nAlso confirmed the prediction from earlier: the \"trim\" clause in the issue is dead weight — stripping leading/trailing `-` handles surrounding whitespace for free. The test still earns its place as a behavioral guarantee.\n\nNothing committed. README doesn't enumerate functions, so no doc change was needed."}],"c